\begin{abstract}
We study temporal dynamics during transformer grokking on modular
arithmetic by directly measuring three distinct milestones in the
\emph{same} training runs:
single-harmonic logit Fourier alignment onset~$\taucirc$
(a logit-level proxy for circuit formation),
generalization onset~$\taug$ (test accuracy), and
embedding-geometry consolidation~$\tauf$ (token-embedding Fourier
alignment).
We provide direct empirical evidence for the full three-stage
ordering $\taucirc<\taug<\tauf$ across two independent training
sweeps with the same nominal seeds: \textbf{$48/54{=}88.9\%$
(Stage~4, RTX~4080)} and \textbf{$44/55{=}80.0\%$ (E3
cross-hardware replication on an A800)}.
The 9-percentage-point gap between these two single-run point
estimates reflects training-side non-determinism that survives
\texttt{cudnn.deterministic=True} across GPU/CUDA configurations
(Section~\ref{sec:posthoc}, E3).
The C$<$G$<$F rate is also detector-dependent: under our
single-harmonic $\flogit$ proxy it is $88.9\%$ (Stage~4), under
Nanda et al.'s excluded-loss progress measure $63.6\%$, and under
their restricted-loss measure $23.6\%$, so the strong-form claim
``the Fourier circuit forms first'' is contingent on accepting
single-harmonic logit projection as the operational proxy for
circuit formation.
A targeted Fourier-subspace intervention further shows that,
between $\taucirc$ and $\tauf$, removing the dominant top-$3$
Fourier harmonics from the logits causes large test-accuracy
drops ($0.58$--$0.72$) while matched-energy random subspace
removals have negligible effect, supporting the causal relevance
of the logit-Fourier proxy rather than a correlated readout
(Section~\ref{sec:posthoc}, E7).
The two intervals are of comparable magnitude: median
$\taug-\taucirc\approx 6{,}250$~steps and median
$\tauf-\taug\approx 6{,}000$~steps.

We then extend the G$<$F (embedding-geometry-lags-generalization)
result across three additional experimental variations beyond the
Stage~2$+$3 1D sweeps: a dense 7$\times$7 (lr$\times$wd) grid
(109/130, 83.8\%, CI 76.6--89.2\%), a different prime $p{=}97$
(21/21, 100\%, CI 84.5--100\%), and modular multiplication via
discrete-log reindexing (30/30, 100\%, CI 88.6--100\%).
Aggregating across all settings, G$<$F holds in 212 of 236 Grokking
runs (\textbf{89.8\%, CI 85.3--93.1\%}); a targeted slow-grokking
sweep at 24 additional cells densely covering the predicted F$<$G
margin ($\taug$ up to $54{,}500$~steps) yields $24/24$ G$<$F and
falsifies the strong form of the speed-dependent ordering
hypothesis (Section~\ref{sec:speed}), bringing the post-hoc
aggregate to $236/260{=}90.8\%$.
The two original Stage~3 F$<$G exceptions do not replicate under
the cross-hardware re-run and we therefore reframe them as
seed/hardware-level fluctuations rather than a systematic regime.
The familiar Fourier ring in PCA visualisations of grokked
embeddings therefore reflects the \emph{third} stage of training---a
post-generalization structural consequence---not the first.
\end{abstract}

