\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Grokking---the abrupt onset of generalization long after training
accuracy saturates---has become a canonical testbed for studying
delayed representation learning in neural networks
\cite{power2022grokking}.
Subsequent work has both extended grokking far beyond toy modular
arithmetic, documenting it on broader architectures and datasets
\cite{liu2023omnigrok,humayun2024deep,notsawo2024beyond}, and
developed mechanistic and theoretical accounts of \emph{when} and
\emph{why} the transition occurs
\cite{nanda2023progress,varma2023explaining,lyu2024dichotomy,
       rubin2024grokking,kumar2024lazy,mohamadi2024grok}.
On modular arithmetic tasks, Nanda et al.~\cite{nanda2023progress} showed that
grokked transformers implement a Fourier circuit: the network uses
trigonometric identities over a small set of task-relevant harmonics
to compute modular addition.
As a consequence, the $p$ token embeddings form a near-circular ring
in PCA space, with embedding~$i$ at angle $2\pi i/p$.

Nanda et al.~\cite{nanda2023progress} also introduced mechanism-level
\emph{progress measures}---quantities such as the Fourier-squared
loss---and found that these measures improve during a circuit-formation
phase that \emph{precedes} the generalization jump.
This raises a natural question about a related but distinct quantity:
the token \emph{embedding geometry} itself.
Does embedding-level Fourier alignment also consolidate before
generalization, or does it lag behind?

Answering this requires measuring the relative timing of two events:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\taug$: generalization onset---the first checkpoint where
    test accuracy $\geq 0.9$ and remains there for three consecutive
    evaluations;
  \item $\tauf$: embedding-geometry onset---the step at which token
    embeddings first show significantly Fourier-aligned structure
    above a permutation-null baseline (BIC changepoint on
    null-corrected Fourier $R^2$).
\end{itemize}

Prior work has not directly measured $\Dtau = \tauf - \taug$.
Nanda et al.~\cite{nanda2023progress} studied mechanism-level dynamics but did not
separately track embedding geometry.
Liu et al.~\cite{liu2022towards} built a static phase diagram but did not
measure within-run event timing.
Our work closes this gap.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
We make three principal contributions.
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{The first direct measurement of all three timing
    milestones in the same training runs, where the circuit-side
    milestone is a logit-level single-harmonic Fourier proxy
    rather than a mechanistic circuit verification.}
    By computing logit-level Fourier alignment $\flogit$ alongside
    the embedding-level $\fcorr$ at every checkpoint, we recover
    $(\taucirc,\taug,\tauf)$ from a single trajectory and establish
    the \emph{full} three-stage ordering $\taucirc<\taug<\tauf$ in
    two independent realizations of the same nominal Stage-4
    design: $48/54{=}88.9\%$ (Stage~4 primary, 95\% CI
    77.8--94.8\%) and $44/55{=}80.0\%$ (E3 cross-hardware
    replication on a different GPU/CUDA configuration; the
    9-percentage-point gap documents an observed cross-hardware
    discrepancy rather than estimating a variance distribution,
    Section~\ref{sec:posthoc}, E3).
    Under coarser loss-based progress measures (Nanda et al.'s
    excluded and restricted-loss) the C$<$G$<$F rate falls
    further, to $63.6\%$ and $23.6\%$ respectively, so the
    headline statement is detector-dependent rather than universal.
    The two intervals $\taug-\taucirc$ and $\tauf-\taug$ are of
    comparable magnitude ($\approx 6{,}000$--$6{,}250$~steps each),
    so the total span $\approx 13{,}000$~steps splits evenly between
    circuit-to-generalization and generalization-to-embedding stages.

  \item \textbf{G$<$F is a robust empirical regularity, not an
    artefact of a single hyperparameter setting.}
    Across the \emph{main stages only} (Stages~2$+$3, 5A, 5B, 6),
    embedding-geometry consolidation lags generalization in
    $212/236$ Grokking runs ($89.8\%$, CI $85.3$--$93.1\%$),
    spanning 1D weight-decay and learning-rate sweeps ($52/55$,
    CI $85.1$--$98.1\%$; Stages~2$+$3), a dense 2D
    (lr$\,\times\,$wd) grid ($109/130$, CI $76.6$--$89.2\%$;
    Stage~6), a different prime $p{=}97$ ($21/21$, CI $84.5$--$100\%$;
    Stage~5B), and modular multiplication via discrete-log
    reindexing ($30/30$, CI $88.6$--$100\%$; Stage~5A).
    The original F$<$G exceptions concentrate at phase boundaries
    with unusually slow generalization and motivated a
    speed-dependent ordering hypothesis; we test the strong form
    of that hypothesis directly in Section~\ref{sec:speed} and
    falsify it with a targeted slow-grokking sweep
    (24/24 fresh G$<$F cells), bringing the aggregate
    \emph{including the targeted slow-grokking sweep} to
    $236/260 = 90.8\%$.

  \item \textbf{A principled, robust detector for $\tauf$.}
    We introduce a null-corrected Fourier alignment metric
    $\fcorr(t)=\max(0,F_{\mathrm{raw}}(t)-F_{\mathrm{null,p95}}(t))$
    that removes the high-dimensional chance-alignment baseline, and
    pair it with a BIC-selected segmented-regression changepoint
    estimator.
    A 36-configuration sensitivity study yields a mean robustness
    score of 0.94 across seven Grokking cells, and the raw vs.\
    null-corrected metrics produce \emph{identical orderings} on all
    8 sensitivity cells with $|\Dtau|\geq 3{,}000$~steps, so the main
    finding is not driven by detector hyperparameters.
\end{enumerate}

Our results establish a \textbf{three-stage timing picture of
grokking}:
(1)~single-harmonic logit Fourier alignment onset $\taucirc$, a
logit-level proxy for circuit formation,
(2)~generalization onset $\taug$, and
(3)~embedding-geometry consolidation $\tauf$.
All three timescales are measured directly in the same runs.
The familiar Fourier ring visible in PCA visualizations reflects
stage~(3), a post-generalization structural consequence, rather
than stage~(1), whose rise precedes the generalization jump.

