\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We have directly measured all three stages of a three-stage timing
picture of grokking in the same training runs: single-harmonic
logit Fourier alignment onset $\taucirc$ (a logit-level proxy for
circuit formation), generalization onset $\taug$, and
embedding-geometry consolidation $\tauf$.
Across two independent realizations of the same nominal Stage-4
design, the full C$<$G$<$F ordering rate ranges from $44/55$
($80.0\%$, E3 cross-hardware replication) to $48/54$
($88.9\%$, Stage~4 primary), with approximately equal gaps
between consecutive stages ($\approx 6{,}000$--$6{,}250$ steps
each); the 9-percentage-point gap documents an observed
cross-hardware discrepancy rather than estimating a variance
distribution.
Under coarser loss-based proxies for circuit formation (Nanda
et al.'s excluded and restricted-loss progress measures) the
C$<$G$<$F rate falls further, to $63.6\%$ and $23.6\%$
respectively (Section~\ref{sec:posthoc}, E3), so the headline
statement is detector-dependent rather than universal.

Across 236~Grokking runs spanning two tasks, two primes, and a dense
2D hyperparameter grid, 89.8\% show G$<$F ordering; the targeted
slow-grokking sweep of Section~\ref{sec:speed} adds 24 fresh cells
that uniformly satisfy G$<$F and falsifies the strong form of the
speed-dependent ordering hypothesis, bringing the post-hoc
aggregate to $236/260{=}90.8\%$.
\textbf{No Grokking run in the weight-decay slice (Stage~2) or in
the cross-task slices (Stage~5A, Stage~5B) reverses the ordering
to F$<$G}: Stage~2 is 29/30~G$<$F with 1/30 coincident
($\Dtau{=}0$, treated as a tie), Stage~5A is 30/30~G$<$F, and
Stage~5B is 21/21~G$<$F.
The learning-rate slice (Stage~3) contains two original
phase-boundary F$<$G exceptions at the slow-grokking margin
($\taug\geq 43{,}500$~steps), but the cross-hardware re-run on
$(\mathrm{lr},\mathrm{seed}){=}(6.8\!\times\!10^{-4}, 7)$ within
the slow-grokking sweep replicates as G$<$F (Section~\ref{sec:speed});
we therefore reframe these as seed/hardware-level fluctuations
rather than a systematic regime transition.
The remaining $21/130$ ($16.2\%$) non-G$<$F runs on the full 2D
grid (Stage~6) consist of
$13$ F$<$G, $3$ coincident ($|\Dtau|\leq500$), and $5$ G\_only
($\tauf$ not detected) cases.
Median embedding-geometry lag ranges from $\approx6{,}000$~steps
($p{=}53$) to $28{,}000$~steps ($p{=}97$), scaling with the
difficulty of the task.

The familiar Fourier ring in grokking PCA visualizations reflects
the \emph{third} stage---a post-generalization structural
consequence---not the \emph{first} (circuit formation), which
drives the generalization jump.

A methodological takeaway: raw Fourier alignment can shift the estimated
onset earlier, but in our sensitivity cells it does not reverse the
ordering sign ($0/8$ flips; Table~\ref{tab:raw_corr}).
Null correction is nonetheless important for principled calibration,
as it prevents false positives near the coincident boundary where
$F_{\mathrm{raw}}$ reflects high-dimensional chance alignment rather
than genuine Fourier structure.

\paragraph{Broader impact.}
This work studies a small-scale neural network training phenomenon.
No immediate harmful applications are foreseen.
Findings may inform training efficiency research by clarifying which
structural changes are or are not necessary for generalization.

