\section{Future Experiments}
\label{app:future_experiments}

The following experiments are the natural next steps required to
(a)~tighten the three-stage picture from empirical to mechanistic
and (b)~test the speed-dependent ordering hypothesis of
Section~\ref{sec:speed}.
The Nanda-comparison post-hoc E3, the decoupled weight-decay
ablation E4, the data-subsampling speed control E5, and the
bootstrap-CI post-hoc E6 have since been executed and are
reported in the E3 paragraph of Section~\ref{sec:posthoc},
Section~\ref{sec:e4_ablation}, Section~\ref{sec:e5_subsample},
and the E6 paragraph of Section~\ref{sec:posthoc},
respectively.
The remaining items below are the natural next steps still to be
run; we list them here so that the reader can distinguish what
\emph{has} been measured from what \emph{would} be measured in a
strictly causal test.
E1 and E2 extend the empirical scope but do not themselves alter
any causal claim.

\paragraph{E1. Full subtraction sweep.}
Replicate Stages~2 and~3 with operation $(a-b)\bmod p$ for
$p\in\{53, 97\}$.
Expected cost: $\sim$60 runs, $\sim$45~GPU-hours.
Predicted outcome under the speed-dependent hypothesis: the F$<$G
rate should track $\taug$, not the task label.
E1 broadens task coverage and can be deferred if compute is
tight.

\paragraph{E2. Architecture-depth scan.}
Sweep $n_{\mathrm{layers}}\in\{1,2,4,6\}$ at fixed (lr, wd).
Expected cost: $\sim$36 runs, $\sim$30~GPU-hours.
Purpose: check whether the C$<$G$<$F ordering is specific to the
2-layer setting used throughout this paper.
Like E1, E2 is an architectural generalisation check rather than a
causal test.

